WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.609 --> 00:00:21.134
year, small birds called Arctic terns accomplish the amazing feat of crossing the globe from north to south.

2
00:00:21.814 --> 00:00:28.039
Every year, small eel larvae leave their birthplace and swim two thousand kilometers, even though they are only

3
00:00:28.059 --> 00:00:29.920
1.5 centimeters long!

4
00:00:30.852 --> 00:00:36.694
Animal migration is the movement of organisms from one area to another and usually back again.

5
00:00:37.574 --> 00:00:40.695
Many animals migrate, but on different time scales.

6
00:00:41.555 --> 00:00:46.617
Migration may happen over a few months, as in the spring and fall migration of many birds.

7
00:00:47.537 --> 00:00:50.938
Or, it may take a lifetime, as in the migration of salmon.

8
00:00:51.798 --> 00:00:56.840
As adults, these fish return to the same streams where they were hatched in order to spawn.

9
00:00:57.596 --> 00:01:01.160
The many mysteries of migration have long baffled scientists.

10
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.244
But the answers to some of the most puzzling questions are known.

11
00:01:06.385 --> 00:01:09.528
Most animals are happy to live their lives in the same place.

12
00:01:10.429 --> 00:01:13.512
Why do some of them embark on long and dangerous journeys?

13
00:01:14.313 --> 00:01:16.355
The first reason is changes in the weather.

14
00:01:17.035 --> 00:01:21.620
In the warmer seasons, food is usually plentiful, whereas in winter, food is scarce.

15
00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:27.431
So, in order to find enough food to survive, some animals must move to warmer climates.

16
00:01:28.212 --> 00:01:30.753
The second reason animals migrate is to breed.

17
00:01:31.574 --> 00:01:38.038
Migrating animals usually return to the same spot where their ancestors reproduced because it is safe and has

18
00:01:38.118 --> 00:01:40.359
enough food for them to raise their own offspring.

19
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.341
How do animals know when it is time to leave?

20
00:01:44.141 --> 00:01:47.924
Both external and internal signals tell an animal when to migrate.

21
00:01:48.784 --> 00:01:56.590
External changes, like a scarcity of food or colder weather, can start migration, and internal signals, caused by

22
00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:00.153
certain hormones, can also tell an animal when it's time to go.

23
00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:06.678
Once they have started on their journeys, animals use a variety of special abilities to guide them to

24
00:02:06.718 --> 00:02:07.778
their destinations.

25
00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:13.283
At the simplest level, animals rely on the currents of the wind or ocean to guide them.

26
00:02:14.023 --> 00:02:19.865
For example, sparrows in eastern North America catch winds that carry them to South America.

27
00:02:20.626 --> 00:02:26.307
Other animals use familiar landmarks, like coastlines, mountain ranges, and rivers, to navigate.

28
00:02:27.268 --> 00:02:30.669
Animals may also rely on the sun and the stars for direction.

29
00:02:31.449 --> 00:02:37.211
The way the sun's rays refract in the water helps fish to swim in the right direction, while

30
00:02:37.291 --> 00:02:41.612
birds that migrate at night use the position of the stars to guide their journeys.

31
00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:48.283
Some birds, notably pigeons, have the ability to find their destination even if they are taken far off

32
00:02:48.323 --> 00:02:56.528
course. Scientists have discovered tiny crystals of magnetite, a magnetic material, in their brains, and they believe these

33
00:02:56.608 --> 00:03:00.050
crystals might help them use the earth's magnetic field as a guide.

34
00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:05.553
Most species, however, use a combination of these navigation techniques when they migrate.

35
00:03:06.493 --> 00:03:14.125
For some animals, like monarch butterflies, migration routes are innate, meaning that the butterflies are born already knowing

36
00:03:14.165 --> 00:03:15.045
the path to take.

37
00:03:15.885 --> 00:03:21.547
Every fall, when the weather gets colder, adult monarchs leave the Rocky Mountains in the United States and

38
00:03:21.587 --> 00:03:27.569
Canada and migrate to central Mexico, as far as 2,900 kilometers away.

39
00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:33.370
The butterflies fly at such high altitudes that they are invisible to the human eye.

40
00:03:34.171 --> 00:03:40.605
When they arrive in Mexico, groups of butterflies congregate in the same place, and trees may be completely

41
00:03:40.665 --> 00:03:41.426
covered with them.

42
00:03:42.247 --> 00:03:48.352
As the winter draws to a close, the butterflies mate and then fly north again, laying eggs along

43
00:03:48.392 --> 00:03:48.812
the way.

44
00:03:49.633 --> 00:03:55.377
The eggs hatch, grow, and then travel northward, following the same migration path as their parents.

45
00:03:56.678 --> 00:04:01.562
Many other animals, like Arctic terns, must teach their young their migratory routes.

46
00:04:02.403 --> 00:04:08.248
The Arctic tern is the most famous migratory bird because it travels halfway around the world and back,

47
00:04:08.688 --> 00:04:14.173
approximately 22,000 kilometers every year─the longest distance for any bird.

48
00:04:14.913 --> 00:04:19.977
It migrates from its breeding grounds in the Arctic to the other side of the world, Antarctica.

49
00:04:20.918 --> 00:04:23.860
Arctic terns are social birds that live in large groups.

50
00:04:24.741 --> 00:04:30.826
Right before migration, the noisy terns suddenly get quiet, and then they all take off and begin their

51
00:04:30.866 --> 00:04:31.867
three-month journey.

52
00:04:32.788 --> 00:04:37.952
Along the way, terns sustain themselves by swooping into the ocean to catch fish to eat.

53
00:04:38.684 --> 00:04:43.465
Young Arctic terns migrate south with their parents, but are left behind in the spring.

54
00:04:44.166 --> 00:04:48.567
They live in Antarctica for two years, and then, they return to the Arctic on their own.

55
00:04:49.527 --> 00:04:52.528
They learned the way from their parents and will teach it to their young.

56
00:04:53.508 --> 00:04:57.589
Scientists believe that some species navigate by using their sense of smell.

57
00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:04.191
For example, certain types of Pacific and Atlantic salmon return to the exact spot in the river where

58
00:05:04.211 --> 00:05:10.137
they were born by somehow sniffing it out, even after spending almost their entire lives in the ocean.

59
00:05:11.058 --> 00:05:17.220
They make this long journey because female salmon must migrate back to their birthplace before they release their

60
00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:23.363
eggs. Some salmon migrate hundreds, even thousands, of kilometers to reach their breeding grounds.

61
00:05:24.203 --> 00:05:30.406
After spawning, the female salmon usually die in the same stream in which they were born─a true cycle

62
00:05:30.446 --> 00:05:30.906
of life.

63
00:05:31.666 --> 00:05:38.369
The salmon's annual return to certain rivers is so reliable that many Native Americans on the western coasts

64
00:05:38.409 --> 00:05:43.390
of America and Canada hold a ceremony to honor the salmon when they return each year.

65
00:05:44.251 --> 00:05:50.232
These people catch the salmon for food, so for them, these migratory fish are a symbol of Nature's

66
00:05:50.292 --> 00:05:51.513
ability to provide.

67
00:05:52.593 --> 00:05:55.534
Other fish use currents to direct them during their migration.

68
00:05:56.274 --> 00:06:01.696
European eels actually start their lives as larvae in the Sargasso Sea, in the western part of the

69
00:06:01.736 --> 00:06:02.516
Atlantic Ocean.

70
00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:09.363
Despite being only 1.5 centimeters long, the larvae are carried all the way to Europe on the Gulf

71
00:06:09.403 --> 00:06:12.384
Stream, a current that flows across the Atlantic.

72
00:06:13.185 --> 00:06:16.406
This journey takes anywhere from seven months to three years.

73
00:06:17.306 --> 00:06:24.129
To reach freshwater rivers inland, the young eels overcome all sorts of challenges: they dig through wet sand,

74
00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:29.452
slide through wet grass, and even pile their bodies on top of each other to climb over obstacles!

75
00:06:30.548 --> 00:06:36.452
Once in freshwater, the male and female eels live up to twenty years and fifty years, respectively.

76
00:06:37.312 --> 00:06:42.155
When the adult eels are ready to breed, they must make the long journey back to the Sargasso

77
00:06:42.215 --> 00:06:45.697
Sea. Eels change their bodies especially for this trip.

78
00:06:46.498 --> 00:06:51.260
Before an adult eel begins to migrate, its eyes grow to allow it to see in the dim

79
00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:56.964
light of the ocean, and its body turns a silver color that makes it nearly invisible to predators.

80
00:06:58.108 --> 00:07:03.050
While many animals migrate at least once in their lives, they do not always survive.

81
00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:07.572
Migration is full of hazards, both natural and human made.

82
00:07:08.292 --> 00:07:15.035
Natural hazards include changes in the weather, scarcity of food, drought, predators, and the physical demands of the

83
00:07:15.075 --> 00:07:15.795
long journey.

84
00:07:16.555 --> 00:07:20.037
Human constructions can also be hazardous to migratory animals.

85
00:07:20.897 --> 00:07:27.500
Skyscrapers and radio towers block the paths of migrating birds, while dams block the rivers that fish travel.

86
00:07:28.204 --> 00:07:33.827
Through their research, scientists are learning how people can minimize their interference with migratory routes.

87
00:07:34.527 --> 00:07:40.610
There are still many aspects of migration that scientists do not understand and mysteries to be revealed.

88
00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:47.293
But that won't stop millions of animals from making their amazing journeys─just as they have been doing for

89
00:07:47.373 --> 00:07:48.353
thousands of years.
